Relevant columns and why they matter  
• `year`:           to limit the analysis to 2010‑2014 and to report the result year  
• `month`:          to aggregate by calendar month and report the result month  
• `air_temperature`:       one of the four temperatures whose monthly mean is required  
• `wetbulb_temperature`:   one of the four temperatures whose monthly mean is required  
• `dewpoint_temperature`:  one of the four temperatures whose monthly mean is required  
• `sea_surface_temp`:      one of the four temperatures whose monthly mean is required  

Typical conditions that will be applied  
• `year BETWEEN 2010 AND 2014` – restrict the five yearly tables to the study period  
• the four temperature columns **IS NOT NULL** – ensure each record used in the mean contains every temperature needed  
• optional quality checks such as `ncdc_qc_flags IS NULL` or record‑count thresholds can be added, but are not strictly required for the basic answer  

Below are ten exploratory/final BigQuery SQL statements (each different, all `SELECT` only, each limited to 20 rows).  
Replace or append additional logic as needed; the last two queries show how to arrive at the requested top‑3 result.

```sql
--Description: 1) Quick look at temperature values in 2010
SELECT year, month, air_temperature, wetbulb_temperature, dewpoint_temperature, sea_surface_temp
FROM `bigquery-public-data.noaa_icoads.icoads_core_2010`
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
--Description: 2) Same quick look for 2011
SELECT year, month, air_temperature, wetbulb_temperature, dewpoint_temperature, sea_surface_temp
FROM `bigquery-public-data.noaa_icoads.icoads_core_2011`
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
--Description: 3) Distinct year‑month pairs having *all* four temps in 2012
